Recommends: wtype | ydotool | xdotool,
            wl-clipboard | xclip | xsel
Suggests: sway, hyprland
Description: voice dictation that types where you are looking
 Press a key, speak, press it again, and the words are typed into whatever
 window has focus. The daemon holds one speech model for the whole session, so
 a dictation costs the time it takes to say it and not the time it takes to
 load a model.
 .
 Speech recognition runs locally on the CPU or, where one is present, a Vulkan
 GPU; no audio leaves the machine and nothing needs a network after the model
 has been fetched. Models are downloaded on request into the user's cache,
 never at install time.
 .
 It can also transcribe a recording into a document that says who spoke, by
 clustering speaker embeddings rather than by a model with a fixed number of
 speakers in it, so a recording of a dozen people is a document about a dozen
 people.
 .
 Typing has no portable tool on Linux: wtype covers the wlroots compositors
 (sway, Hyprland, river, wayfire, labwc, niri), ydotool covers GNOME and KDE
 through the kernel's uinput device, and xdotool covers X11. The clipboard,
 which the paste methods borrow, is wl-clipboard on Wayland and xclip or xsel
 on X11. Install whichever suits the desktop -- diktat tries them in order and
 says which are missing.
